    COMPOSABLE ARCHITECTURE

    The key insight is separating three concerns:

    ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │  struct cg_vtable                                           │
    │  ~90 function pointers for code generation                  │
    │  Shared by ALL targets of same CPU architecture             │
    │  Example: cg_386_vtable used by Linux, FreeBSD, Windows     │
    └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                               │
                               ▼
    ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │  struct cg_arch                                             │
    │  CPU properties: bits, endian, registers, directives        │
    │  Shared by ALL targets of same CPU architecture             │
    │  Example: cg_386_arch used by Linux, FreeBSD, Windows       │
    └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                               │
                               ▼
    ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │  struct cg_os_config                                        │
    │  OS properties: asm_cmd, ld_cmd, obj_format                 │
    │  Shared by ALL targets on same OS                           │
    │  Example: cg_os_linux_elf32 used by 386-linux               │
    └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                               │
                               ▼
    ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │  struct cg_target                                           │
    │  Final target: combines arch + os + vtable via pointers     │
    │  Very small: just name, description, and 3 pointers         │
    └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘


    BEFORE vs AFTER

    BEFORE (cg_386_linux.c): ~300 lines
        struct cg_target target_386_linux = {
            "386-linux",
            "Intel 386 (Linux)",
            32, 1, 2, 4, 4, 4, 4,           /* sizes */
            ENDIAN_LITTLE, STACK_DOWN, ...   /* arch props */
            OS_LINUX, OBJ_ELF, 0, ...        /* os props */
            "%eax", "%ecx", ...              /* registers */
            ".text", ".data", ...            /* directives */
            cg386_data, cg386_text, ...      /* ~90 function ptrs */
        };

    AFTER (cg_386_linux.c): ~20 lines
        struct cg_target target_386_linux = {
            "386-linux",
            "Intel 386 (Linux)",
            &cg_386_arch,           /* shared arch properties */
            &cg_os_linux_elf32,     /* shared OS config */
            &cg_386_vtable,         /* shared vtable */
            NULL, NULL, NULL        /* overrides */
        };


    ADDING A NEW TARGET

    For a new OS on existing architecture (e.g., 386-myos):

    1. Add OS config to cg_os_configs.c (or reuse existing):
           struct cg_os_config cg_os_myos = { ... };

    2. Create target file (cg_386_myos.c):
           #include "cg_386_arch.h"
           #include "cg_os_configs.h"
           struct cg_target target_386_myos = {
               "386-myos", "Intel 386 (MyOS)",
               &cg_386_arch, &cg_os_myos, &cg_386_vtable,
               NULL, NULL, NULL
           };

    3. Register in cgtarget.c:
           extern struct cg_target target_386_myos;
           register_target(&target_386_myos);

    For a new architecture (e.g., RISC-V):

    1. Create code generator: cg_riscv.c
       - Implement all ~90 code generation functions

    2. Create function header: cg_riscv_funcs.h
       - Declare all function prototypes

    3. Create arch definition: cg_riscv_arch.c
       - Define cg_riscv_arch with CPU properties
       - Define cg_riscv_vtable pointing to functions

    4. Create arch header: cg_riscv_arch.h
       - Declare extern cg_riscv_arch and cg_riscv_vtable

    5. Create OS targets: cg_riscv_linux.c, etc.
       - Each is just ~20 lines combining arch + os + vtable

    6. Register all targets in cgtarget.c


    TARGET STRUCTURE DETAILS

    struct cg_vtable:
        ~90 function pointers for all code generation operations.
        Grouped by category: section control, loads, stores,
        arithmetic, comparisons, branches, etc.

    struct cg_arch:
        bits            Architecture bit width (16, 32, 64)
        char_size       sizeof(char)
        int_size        sizeof(int)
        ptr_size        sizeof(void*)
        bpw             Bytes Per Word
        endian          ENDIAN_LITTLE or ENDIAN_BIG
        stack_dir       STACK_DOWN or STACK_UP
        asm_syntax      ASM_GAS, ASM_TASM, ASM_HLASM, ASM_ARM
        call_conv       CC_CDECL, CC_SYSV_AMD64, CC_HLASM, etc.
        float_format    FLOAT_IEEE754, FLOAT_IBM_HEX, etc.
        
        /* Stack Frame Layout (new in v2.1) */
        param_offset_base   Initial offset for first parameter
        param_offset_dir    0 = follows stack_dir, 1 = always positive
        local_offset_base   Initial offset for local variables
        stack_slot_size     Size of each stack slot (0 = use BPW)
                            ARM64 uses 16 for 16-byte stack alignment
        
        /* Symbol Transformation (new in v2.1) */
        symbol_transform    Callback to transform symbol names (or NULL)

    struct cg_os_config:
        target_os       OS_LINUX, OS_FREEBSD, OS_DOS, OS_MVS, etc.
        obj_format      OBJ_ELF, OBJ_MACHO, OBJ_PE, OBJ_OMF, OBJ_GOFF
        underscore_sym  1 if symbols need underscore prefix
        os_name         Display name
        asm_cmd         Assembler command template
        ld_cmd          Linker command template
        ld_cmd_sysrt    Linker command for system runtime (-R flag)
        sys_libc        System C library flags
        aout_name       Default output filename

    struct cg_vtable (partial - system runtime support):
        cgmovearg(n)    Move accumulator to argument register n (0-7)
        maxregargs      Maximum number of register arguments (0 = stack only)

    struct cg_target:
        name            Short name for --target flag
        description     Human-readable description
        arch            Pointer to cg_arch (shared)
        os              Pointer to cg_os_config (shared)
        vtable          Pointer to cg_vtable (shared)
        asm_cmd_override Override OS asm_cmd if needed
        ld_cmd_override  Override OS ld_cmd if needed
        ext_data        Extension data for special needs


    ARM64/AARCH64 TARGETS

    The ARM64 target generates GNU Assembler code for 64-bit ARM
    processors. Key characteristics:

    - 64-bit registers and pointers
    - Little-endian byte order
    - Stack grows downward (STACK_DOWN)
    - 16-byte stack alignment required
    - IEEE 754 floating point
    - GAS assembly syntax

    Two OS targets are supported:

    arm64-linux   - ARM64 Linux (ELF)
    arm64-darwin  - ARM64 Darwin/macOS (Mach-O)

    The architecture definition includes ARM64-specific settings:
    - stack_slot_size = 16 (16-byte stack alignment)
    - All stack operations aligned to 16 bytes
    - Uses x0 as accumulator, x1 as auxiliary register
    - Frame pointer in x29, link register in x30

    Register usage:
    - x0       Primary accumulator, return value
    - x1       Auxiliary register for binary operations
    - x2, x3   Scratch registers
    - x29      Frame pointer
    - x30      Link register (return address)
    - sp       Stack pointer (must be 16-byte aligned)

    System Runtime Mode (-R flag):
    ARM64 supports the -R flag for linking with system libc. When active:
    - cgemitargs() handles argument emission per AAPCS64 convention
    - Fixed arguments go in x0-x7 registers
    - Variadic arguments go on stack (as required by AAPCS64)
    - Both variadic (printf) and non-variadic (write) functions work


    IBM SYSTEM/370 TARGETS

    The S/370 target generates HLASM (High Level Assembler) code for
    IBM mainframes running MVS. Key characteristics:

    - Big-endian architecture
    - Stack grows upward (STACK_UP)
    - IBM Hexadecimal floating point (FLOAT_IBM_HEX)
    - HLASM assembly syntax
    - GOFF object format
    - MVS calling convention (CC_HLASM)

    Two variants are supported:

    s370     - Original System/370 with 24-bit addressing
    s370-xa  - System/370-XA with 31-bit addressing

    The architecture definition includes MVS-specific settings:
    - param_offset_base = 0 (params via R11)
    - param_offset_dir = 1 (always positive offsets)
    - local_offset_base = 88 (after save area)
    - symbol_transform = s370_symbol_transform (uppercase, 8-char limit)

    The code generator produces MVS-compatible output with:
    - COPY PDPTOP header
    - CSECT directive
    - LTORG for literal pools
    - Page tables (@@PGT0, @@PGT1, etc.)
    - Standard MVS prologue/epilogue

    Example output:
             COPY  PDPTOP
             CSECT
    * Program text area
             DS    0F
    FUNC     B     L1
             LTORG
    L1:      DROP  12
             BALR  12,0
             USING *,12
             LR    11,1
             ...


    ARM64/AARCH64 TARGETS

    The ARM64 target generates GNU Assembler (GAS) code for 64-bit ARM
    processors. Key characteristics:

    - 64-bit registers and pointers
    - Little-endian byte order (default)
    - Stack grows downward (STACK_DOWN)
    - 16-byte stack alignment required
    - IEEE 754 floating point
    - AAPCS64 calling convention

    Register usage:
      x0      - Primary accumulator, return value
      x1      - Secondary accumulator
      x2      - Tertiary register (pointer for stores)
      x3      - Scratch register
      x29     - Frame pointer (FP)
      x30     - Link register (LR)
      sp      - Stack pointer

    Two OS variants are supported:

    arm64-linux   - Linux with ELF object format
    arm64-darwin  - Darwin/macOS with Mach-O object format

    Darwin differences:
    - Symbols require underscore prefix (_main vs main)
    - Uses @PAGE/@PAGEOFF for PC-relative addressing
    - Mach-O specific section directives

    Linux differences:
    - Uses :pg_hi21:/:lo12: for PC-relative addressing
    - Standard ELF format

    Example output (Linux):
        .arch armv8-a
        .text
        .globl  Cmain
        .align 3
        Cmain:
        stp     x29, x30, [sp, #-16]!
        mov     x29, sp
        mov     x0, #42
        ldp     x29, x30, [sp], #16
        ret

    Example output (Darwin):
        .section __TEXT,__text,regular,pure_instructions
        .build_version macos, 11, 0
        .globl  _Cmain
        .p2align 3
        _Cmain:
        stp     x29, x30, [sp, #-16]!
        mov     x29, sp
        mov     x0, #42
        ldp     x29, x30, [sp], #16
        ret


    USAGE

    Command line:
        scc -T 386-linux program.c
        scc -T x86-64-freebsd program.c
        scc -T arm64-linux program.c
        scc -T arm64-darwin program.c
        scc -T s370 program.c
        scc -T s370-xa program.c
        scc -L                          # list all targets

    In code:
        #include "cgtarget.h"

        cg_init_targets();
        if (!cg_set_target("386-linux")) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Unknown target\n");
            exit(1);
        }
        /* Now CG points to target_386_linux */
        /* Access properties via CG->arch->bits, CG->os->os_name */
        /* Call functions via CG->vtable->cgadd() */


    COMPATIBILITY

    The cgen_compat.h provides macros for existing code:

        /* Old code works unchanged: */
        cgadd();
        cgpush();

        /* Expands to: */
        CG->vtable->cgadd();
        CG->vtable->cgpush();

        /* Architecture properties: */
        #define BPW (CG->arch->bpw)


    C89 COMPLIANCE

    This framework is pure C89:
    - No designated initializers
    - No inline functions
    - No C99/C11 features
    - Standard structs with function pointers
    - Composition via pointers (not inheritance)


    FILES SUMMARY

    cgtarget.h          Main header with all struct definitions
    cgtarget.c          Target registry and selection
    cgen_compat.h       Backward-compatible macros
    cg_os_configs.h     OS config declarations
    cg_os_configs.c     OS config definitions

    Per architecture:
    cg_<arch>.c         Code generation functions
    cg_<arch>_funcs.h   Function prototypes
    cg_<arch>_arch.h    Arch/vtable declarations
    cg_<arch>_arch.c    Arch/vtable definitions
    cg_<arch>_<os>.c    Target descriptors (very small)


